-- Browser date and time builtins, declared as host imports.
--
-- Waluau runs in the browser, so `os.*` is a thin typed surface over the
-- DOM's `Date` and `performance` clocks rather than a C `time.h` port. The
-- functions here are the date/time subset; process, filesystem, locale, and
-- environment entry points of Lua's `os` library have no browser meaning and
-- are deliberately absent.
--
-- Two clocks, two contracts:
--
--   * `os.time` and `os.date` read wall-clock time as whole seconds since the
--     Unix epoch. Wall-clock time can jump when the machine's clock is set.
--   * `os.clock` reads the monotonic page timer (`performance.now`) as
--     fractional seconds since page start. It never jumps backwards, so it is
--     the one to subtract when measuring elapsed time.
-- Whole seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, truncated toward the epoch.
-- Always UTC: the returned instant does not depend on the machine timezone.
declare function os.time(): f64

-- Seconds between two `os.time` instants, `later - earlier`, matching Lua's
-- argument order. Waluau timestamps are already plain seconds, so this is
-- ordinary subtraction rather than a platform time_t conversion.
declare function os.difftime(later: f64, earlier: f64): f64

-- Fractional seconds on the monotonic page timer. The origin is the page load,
-- not the epoch, so only differences between two readings are meaningful.
declare function os.clock(): f64

-- strftime-style formatting of a wall-clock instant.
--
--   os.date(format)        formats the current time
--   os.date(format, time)  formats `time` seconds after the epoch
--
-- A leading `!` selects UTC; without it the browser's local timezone applies,
-- which makes the result depend on the machine running the program. Prefer the
-- `!` forms wherever output has to be reproducible.
--
-- Supported conversions are Luau's set: `%aAbBcdHIjmMpSUwWxXyYzZ` and `%%`.
-- Any other specifier, including the `E`/`O` locale modifiers, raises
-- "invalid conversion specifier". Text outside a conversion is copied through.
--
-- Pre-epoch instants format normally, because the browser's `Date` represents
-- them: `os.date("!%Y", -1)` is "1969". Lua and Luau instead answer nil below
-- the epoch, so this is a deliberate difference and the reason the result is a
-- plain `string` that concatenates and reformats without a nil check.
--
-- Lua's `"*t"` and `"!*t"` calendar-table formats are not supported. A
-- declared host import carries primitives, and a Waluau function has one
-- return type, so `os.date` always returns a formatted string. Requesting a
-- calendar table raises rather than silently formatting the literal text.
declare function os.date(format: string): string
declare function os.date(format: string, time: f64): string
